# Each phrase announces that the writer is about to say a thing again,
# so the fix makes the first statement clear enough to stand alone and
# drops the phrase. `in other words` counts only with a glued comma,
# since the bare form ends a clause (`restates it in other words`).
# `to be clear` also needs the start of a clause after its comma,
# since the infinitive takes a list of adjectives (`comments to be
# clear, accurate, and readable`). A comma ends Harper's default
# chunk, so the rule reads whole sentences to see past it.
#
# `put simply` and `to put it simply` sit in the puff rule, which
# reports every `simply` but the one before `put`, so each set of
# words draws one finding.
expr main [(in other words,), (simply put), (that is to say), (to be clear, [DET, PRON, PROPN, NOUN])]
let message "Restatement. Make the first statement stand alone, and delete the phrase."
let description "Flags phrases that announce the writer is about to say a thing again."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The summary restates the claim in other words."
allows "Rewrite the comments to be clear, accurate, and readable."
